cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Governance Concentration Beyond Token Allocation: A Protocol-Address-Corrected Cross-Sectional Audit of 52 DePIN and DeFi Protocols"
message: "If you use this code or data, please cite as follows."
type: article
authors:
  - family-names: "Zukowski"
    given-names: "Zach"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-3642-2450"
    email: "zach@tokenization.systems"
    affiliation: "Tokenization Systems"
abstract: >-
  A 52-protocol cross-section documents that initial token allocation design
  is not associated with post-distribution governance concentration (insider
  allocation Pearson r = 0.10, p = 0.49, N = 50; statistically equivalent to
  zero within the powered envelope, TOST p = 0.02). Protocols with generous
  community distributions exhibit concentration patterns similar to those
  with heavy insider allocations. Architectural sector is the robust
  correlate (DePIN more concentrated than DeFi): under the headline
  voter-inclusive staking pass-through treatment the contrast is a medium
  effect (Cohen&apos;s d = 0.65, Mann-Whitney p = 0.028, all 30
  leave-one-out folds significant), with a uniform staking-aggregation
  exclusion as a robustness check (Cohen&apos;s d = 0.75) and a powered
  multivariate model retaining a significant DePIN coefficient.
  Delegation amplifies governance concentration
  across thirteen of eighteen protocols in the voting-layer subsample
  (range 2.5x to 25.6x; mean approximately 6.8x, median 4.4x), with five
  structural exceptions showing dispersion (ENS 0.48x, GMX 0.87x, HNT
  0.26x to 0.39x, JUP 0.12x, and LPT 0.27x); magnitude varies by
  institutional design within sector. The exclusion methodology
  identifies 133 address exclusions across 38 protocols (125 unique
  addresses; five recur across multiple protocols) controlled by
  protocols themselves that appear on holder lists but cannot vote;
  correcting for these changes affected protocols' HHI by up to 18x
  (RENDER at 17.9x; median 2.3x across 32 protocols with complete
  pre-exclusion and post-exclusion HHI logged).
keywords:
  - tokenomics
  - institutional design
  - governance concentration
  - DAO governance
  - DePIN
  - delegation amplification
  - Herfindahl-Hirschman Index
  - burn-rule exclusion
  - polycentric governance
  - exclusion methodology
